; ================================================================
;  System Shock 2023 - Snapdragon X / Adreno (ARM, Prism emulation)
;
;  REWRITTEN 2026-08-11 after measuring 6-7 fps. The previous version of this
;  file chased maximum detail with no framerate measurement in the loop, and it
;  was roughly an order of magnitude too heavy for this machine. The old values
;  are not lost: the heavy version is backed up, and every line cut is listed in
;  the ADD-BACK QUEUE at the bottom with its old value.
;
;  Budget reality: GPU benched 150 against a CPU of 223, at 5120x1440 = 7.4
;  MPixels native, running an emulated x64 D3D12 renderer. Both halves were
;  overcommitted - pixels AND draw calls - so both are cut here.
;
;  Add detail back ONE line at a time with the Steam FPS counter visible.
; ================================================================

; ---- THE FLOOR: render resolution. Biggest single lever by far. ----
;      60% of 5120x1440 = 3072x864, i.e. 2.8x fewer pixels, and the UI still
;      draws at native so text stays sharp.
;      TemporalAA.Upsampling=1 is what makes this acceptable: TAA does the
;      upscale from its own history buffer instead of a spatial filter, so 60%
;      reads far closer to native than the resolution slider would suggest.
;      Verified registered in the shipping exe. There is no FSR or DLSS path
;      here - the DLSS plugin ships but is NVIDIA-only, so TAAU is the upscaler.
;      This lives in [SystemSettings] deliberately: SetBySystemSettingsIni
;      outranks SetByScalability, so it holds even when the game's own menu
;      re-applies sg.ResolutionQuality=100 from ShockSettings.sav.
;      TUNE THIS FIRST. 50 if still short, 70-80 once there is headroom.
;      LOCKED AT 60. Tested 75 on 2026-08-11: 27 fps -> 18 fps. 75% is 1.56x the
;      pixels of 60%, and 27/1.56 = 17.3, so pixel cost scales almost perfectly
;      linearly here. THIS GPU IS PIXEL-BOUND, full stop.
;      The corollary, measured the same day: object-count knobs (DetailMode=2,
;      Shadow.RadiusThreshold=0.01, AO levels, SSR roughness, bloom) were ALL free -
;      three separate tests each held 27 fps. So: spend on OBJECT COUNT, never on
;      PIXELS.
;      Lowered 60 -> 52 on 2026-08-11 to buy a stable 30 with a frame cap. Pixel
;      ratio (52/60)^2 = 0.75, so ~27 avg becomes ~36 avg and the floor clears 30.
;      If it still dips below 30: 50, then 48. Do not go above 60.
;      Raised 52 -> 57 later the same day. The ~36 avg predicted above came back as a
;      MEASURED 36, and the ask was to spend that headroom on detail and settle at 30.
;      Pixel cost is linear here (proven by the 60 -> 75 test), so the landing point is
;      52 * sqrt(36/30) = 56.96. Predicts ~30 avg. Resolution is item 1 in the add-back
;      queue because it beats every detail knob for looks, and it is the ONLY knob with
;      a measured model, so it is the only one that can target a specific fps.
;      If the floor dips under 30: 55, then back to 52.
;      57 MEASURED at 31-33 fps, so 52 -> 57 came in slightly CHEAPER than the pure
;      quadratic predicted (it forecast 30). Raised 57 -> 60 on that measurement.
;      Two real points now: 52 -> 36, 57 -> 32. Fitting fps * SP^2 = const off the newer
;      one gives 60 -> 32 * (57/60)^2 = ~29, and 59 -> exactly 30.
;      Went with 60 because it is the round step this queue always intended (60 -> 70 ->
;      80) and 59 vs 60 is inside measurement noise. NOTE the older session measured
;      60 -> 27; that was a different config, so the fresher point wins - but if 60 comes
;      back at 27-28, that old number was right and 58 is the honest landing spot.
;      Caveat worth remembering: 5% more linear resolution is a SMALL visual step. The
;      remaining headroom at 30 fps is only ~7% of the frame budget, so nothing bought
;      with it will be dramatic. Bigger looks need accepting a lower fps target.
;      60 -> 57 ON 2026-09-08, TO HIT 30 fps. Straight from this file's own model rather than a
;      guess: 27 fps measured at SP 60 gives K = 27 * 60^2 = 97,200, and sqrt(97200/30) = 56.9.
;      That K is the same 97,000 the earlier sessions fitted, which is a good sign the model still
;      holds after the fog, SSR and AO cuts. 57 renders 2918x820 instead of 3072x864.
;      Chosen over cutting more detail because resolution is still the best looks-per-frame line
;      in the file, and 5% less LINEAR resolution is a genuinely small visual step - TAAU
;      reconstructs from history, so it does not read like a 5% res drop.
;      If it lands under 30: 55. If it overshoots and you want detail back: 58.
;      Alternative if you would rather keep the pixels: another fog cut (GridPixelSize 10 -> 12,
;      GridSizeZ 48 -> 40) is worth roughly the same, but it costs the light shafts, which are
;      most of this station's atmosphere. Resolution is the cheaper loss.
;      REVERTED TO 60 the same day: he asked whether 30 is worth chasing and the answer is no -
;      see the pacing argument below. Kept the model note above because it is correct and it is
;      how to hit any future target.
;      WHY 30 IS NOT WORTH IT HERE: the usual reason to want exactly 30 on a 60 Hz panel is that
;      it is an exact half-refresh, so with VSync ON every frame shows for exactly two refreshes
;      and pacing is perfectly even. That payoff does not exist in this setup - VSync is OFF (in
;      ShockSettings.sav) and t.MaxFPS was measured INERT here, so nothing would hold the frame
;      at 30. Worse, an AVERAGE of 30 means dips below it, and with VSync back on those dips
;      would land on 20, which is a bigger judder than anything happening now.
;      So 27 -> 30 is a 10% frame-time change with no pacing benefit, paid for with 5% less
;      linear resolution forever. Not a good trade.
;      *** 60 -> 70 ON 2026-09-11, PAID FOR BY THE AO FIX. ***
;      The measured model, all at 100 % res: fixed ~3.4 ms, and ~79.6 ms of resolution-scaled work
;      in the full config. Cutting AO from 29.6 to roughly 7 ms takes that scaled part to ~57 ms.
;      At 70 % the scaled cost is 57 * 0.49 = ~28 ms, plus 3.4 fixed = ~31 ms, i.e. about 32 fps -
;      so this buys BOTH a sharper image than the old 60 % AND the 30 fps that was never reachable
;      while AO was eating a third of the frame.
;      If it lands short of 30: 65, then 60. If it comes back above 35: 80.
;      *** BACK TO 60 ON 2026-09-11 - ERROR, CORRECTED. The AO settings AND the
;      resolution were changed in the same edit, so the 22 fps that came back cannot be attributed to either.
;      60 is the exact resolution of the known 24-26 fps baseline, so with this restored the ONLY
;      difference from that baseline is the AO fix, and the next number measures it cleanly.
;        clearly above 26 -> the AO fix works and the saving can then be spent on resolution.
;        still 24-26      -> the fix did nothing at this resolution and the 29.6 ms AO measurement
;                            did not survive contact with the real config; investigate why.
;      *** 60 -> 50 ON 2026-09-11, TO BUY A STABLE 30 RATHER THAN AN AVERAGE ONE. ***
;      The stability batch did not move it, which says the dips are not spikes - the frame is simply
;      close to the limit everywhere. A FLOOR of 30 therefore needs an AVERAGE near 35, and that
;      needs ~6 ms, which no remaining quality line can give without gutting the look.
;      Resolution is the only lever left with a measured rate: 60 -> 70 cost 6.5 ms, i.e. 0.65 ms per
;      percentage point, so 60 -> 50 returns ~6.5 ms. Predicts ~35 fps average and a floor near 30.
;      THIS IS A REAL VISUAL COST and the honest trade for the stable 30 asked for repeatedly:
;      2560x720 reconstructed to 5120x1440 instead of 3072x864. TAAU hides a lot of it, but fine
;      detail at distance will soften.
;      LADDER: 55 if this overshoots and you want sharpness back; 48 if the floor still misses 30.
;      Everything else in this file stays - the quality settings are no longer what is in the way.
;      *** 50 -> 55 ON 2026-09-11, SPENDING HEADROOM HE TURNED OUT TO HAVE. ***
;      50 % measured 35-36 fps, not the 25-26 first reported - a misread. So there was never a
;      stability deficit at 50 %, there was ~5 ms of SPARE time above the 30 fps target (35.5 fps =
;      28.2 ms against a 33.3 ms budget), and 60 -> 50 gave up more sharpness than was needed.
;      At the measured 0.65 ms per percentage point, 50 -> 55 spends ~3.3 ms and predicts ~32 fps -
;      which leaves a deliberate margin BELOW the 30 fps cap so the cap can actually hold flat
;      rather than being a ceiling the machine keeps falling through.
;      57 if 32 proves comfortable; back to 50 if the floor dips under 30.
;      *** 55 -> 50 ON 2026-09-11, AND THIS IS THE SETTLING POINT. ***
;      At 55 the frame CAP is demonstrably working - a 29-30 reading against a 30 ceiling is the cap
;      holding - but the dips to 27-28 mean the worst case is ~37 ms against a 33.3 ms budget, i.e.
;      about 4 ms short. 50 is the one resolution with a measured average (35-36 fps = 28.2 ms), so
;      it has ~5 ms of margin and the dips should stay above 30 rather than showing through.
;      THE PRINCIPLE, now that a cap is in place: extra average fps above the cap is WASTED, so the
;      right target is not the highest average but the lowest average whose WORST CASE still clears
;      the cap. Everything beyond that should be spent on looks, not frames.
r.ScreenPercentage=50
;      ---- DYNAMIC RESOLUTION - OPTION, NOT APPLIED. Added 2026-09-11. All four verified registered.
;      The file's own model says resolution is the best looks-per-frame line and that the GPU is
;      pixel-bound. Dynamic resolution spends that: it holds a frame-TIME target and lets the
;      render scale float, so an empty corridor renders at 80-85 % and a fight drops to 55-60 %.
;      It replaces r.ScreenPercentage above while active (the fraction comes from the dynamic
;      state, not the cvar). Mode 2 ignores the game's own bUseDynamicResolution=False.
;      Budget 37 ms = the 27 fps measured at SP 60, so the floor of the current look is kept and
;      the headroom goes to pixels. It needs working GPU timestamps under Prism: if the picture
;      pins to 55 (blurry) or to 85 (fps collapses), the timer is broken - remove the block.
;      It also changes the METHOD in this file: fps becomes flat by design and quality varies,
;      so the K model stops applying while this is on.
;      *** LIVE 2026-09-11. THIS REPLACES r.ScreenPercentage=60 ABOVE - that line is now INERT
;      while dynamic resolution is active, because the render fraction comes from the dynamic
;      state instead of the cvar. It is left in place so that turning this block off restores the
;      known-good 60 in one step.
;      WHAT CHANGES: instead of one fixed resolution chosen for the worst case, the engine holds a
;      frame TIME and floats the resolution to meet it. An empty corridor - which is most of this
;      game - renders well above 60 %, and a fight in smoke drops below it. That is the whole
;      point: the resolution is spent where there is room for it and given back where there is not.
;      BUDGET 37 ms. With TargetedGPUHeadRoomPercentage left at its default 10, the heuristic
;      actually aims at ~33 ms, so it errs toward FRAMES over resolution - deliberate, after
;      21 fps was called bad. Raise the budget to 45 to buy resolution back at ~22 fps.
;      RANGE 50-80 %. 60 was the fixed value, so there is real room in both directions.
;      ChangePercentageThreshold 2 -> 4 so it does not re-scale for a 2 % difference; at 2 the
;      resolution visibly pumps on a GPU whose frame time is this noisy.
;      *** THE RISK, STATED PLAINLY: this heuristic reads GPU TIMESTAMPS every frame, which is the
;      same mechanism just blamed for the translucency regression above. If Prism does not deliver
;      usable timings, this fails in one of two visible ways, and both are one flip to undo:
;        - the picture goes soft and STAYS soft (pinned at 50), or
;        - fps collapses to ~15 and stays there (pinned at 80).
;      Either way set OperationMode back to 0 and r.ScreenPercentage=60 takes over again.
;      *** OPERATION MODE 2 -> 0 ON 2026-09-11. DYNAMIC RESOLUTION IS INERT IN THIS BUILD. ***
;      Proof: the potato test set r.ScreenPercentage=25 with this at 0 and got 240 fps, while every
;      ceiling change made with this at 2 - 40, 70, 80, 100 - changed nothing at all. The game
;      overrides it from code, which is the same SetByCode-beats-ini trap this file already
;      documents for t.MaxFPS and for bUseHDRDisplayOutput.
;      Turning it off explicitly so r.ScreenPercentage above is unambiguously the resolution knob.
;      Every r.DynamicRes.* line below is now decoration - do not tune them, and do not trust any
;      conclusion in this file that was drawn from one.
r.DynamicRes.OperationMode=0
;      *** RETUNED FOR A STABLE 30 fps ON 2026-09-11. Read this before changing anything here.
;      THE DIVISION OF LABOUR, now that dynamic resolution is on: the BUDGET sets the frame rate,
;      and everything else in this file sets how much RESOLUTION that frame rate buys. Chasing fps
;      with detail cuts is the old method and no longer applies - cuts now come back as sharpness.
;      Budget 33.3 ms = 30 fps exactly. With TargetedGPUHeadRoomPercentage at its default 10 the
;      heuristic aims at ~30 ms, i.e. 33 fps, and that margin is what makes 30 a FLOOR rather than
;      an average - which is what "stable 30" actually means.
;      Min 50 -> 40: at 50 the heuristic had nowhere left to go in a heavy fight, so it went over
;      budget and the frame rate sagged instead of the picture. 40 is ugly but it is only reached
;      in the worst moments, and a stable 30 is what was asked for.
;      Max 80 -> 70: a lower ceiling means less distance to fall when a fight starts, so the
;      resolution swings are smaller and the frame time is steadier.
r.DynamicRes.FrameTimeBudget=33.3
;      *** TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC 2026-09-11 - NOT a setting, REVERT AFTER ONE LAUNCH. ***
;      Min and Max are pinned EQUAL so the render fraction cannot move. This is a single-variable
;      test of one question: is the frame rate limited by PIXEL work at all?
;      Three fixed-cost cuts (shadow maps 2048->1024, view distance 1.5->1.0, skeletal LOD -1->0)
;      moved the frame rate by ZERO, and a 10 % cut in the frame-time budget moved it ~4 %. Both
;      say the GPU's pixel work is not what is holding the frame.
;      READ THE RESULT LIKE THIS:
;        fps jumps to ~35+  -> it IS pixel bound, the heuristic was simply not lowering resolution,
;                              and the fix is dynamic-resolution tuning, not more cutting.
;        fps stays 26-29    -> NOT pixel bound. The limit is the render thread translating D3D12
;                              under Prism (it carries the RHI work since -norhithread), or the
;                              full-output-resolution passes at 5120x1440. No graphics cut reaches
;                              30 in that case, and the only real levers left are draw-call count
;                              (r.DetailMode, r.Shadow.RadiusThreshold) or output resolution.
;      Restore 40 / 70 from Engine.ini.bak-2026-09-11f afterwards either way.
;      *** DIAGNOSTIC RESULT 2026-09-11, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT MEASUREMENT IN THIS FILE. ***
;      Pinned at 40 %: 26-28 fps. Floating 40-70 %: 26-28 fps. IDENTICAL. Nearly HALF the pixels
;      changed nothing, which proves the frame is NOT limited by pixel work at all.
;      WHAT WAS REALLY HAPPENING: the heuristic measures GPU time only. The GPU had spare capacity,
;      so it raised the render fraction to its ceiling and sat there - 70 was never a compromise,
;      it was simply the top of the range it was allowed. The limit is elsewhere: the render thread
;      translating D3D12 under Prism emulation, which also carries the RHI work since -norhithread.
;      CONSEQUENCE, and it inverts this file's entire history: PIXEL WORK IS EFFECTIVELY FREE below
;      that floor, and DRAW-CALL work is what actually costs. Every "expensive pixel line" cut over
;      the last month was sold for nothing. The old fps * SP^2 = K model is DEAD - it described a
;      GPU-bound machine, and this one is not GPU bound any more.
;      Max 40 -> 100 so the render fraction climbs until the GPU genuinely becomes the limit. That
;      is now self-balancing: everything restored below competes with resolution for the same
;      headroom, and the heuristic arbitrates automatically.
;      Min 40 -> 50 because the floor should never be reached now; if it is, something regressed.
r.DynamicRes.MinScreenPercentage=50
;      *** 100 -> 80 ON 2026-09-11. THE CEILING IS THE REAL RESOLUTION CONTROL ON THIS MACHINE. ***
;      Three measurements now say the heuristic does not track its budget here - it simply parks at
;      whatever ceiling it is given: max 40 -> 26-28 fps, max 70 -> 26-28 fps, max 100 -> 22-23 fps.
;      The first two are equal because the RENDER THREAD was the limit in both; at 100 % the pixel
;      cost finally overtook it, which is the crossover this session was looking for.
;      So treat MaxScreenPercentage as a fixed screen percentage, and the budget below as inert.
;      WHY 80: at max 100 the frame was ~43 ms. Pixel cost scales with the SQUARE of the fraction,
;      so 80 % is 0.64 of the pixels, i.e. roughly 28 ms of GPU - comfortably back under the
;      ~36-38 ms render-thread floor that produced 26-28 fps. That should restore the frame rate
;      while keeping a HIGHER resolution than the 70 that was running before today's restorations.
;      If it comes back under 26, go to 70. If it comes back above 28, 85 is the next step up -
;      free frames do not exist above the floor, so anything over 28 is unspent resolution.
;      NOTHING ELSE WAS TOUCHED: PCSS, glossy SSR, the 8/64 fog, full AA, full-res translucency and
;      the full AO budget all stay. They are pixel work and resolution is the cheaper thing to sell.
r.DynamicRes.MaxScreenPercentage=80
r.DynamicRes.ChangePercentageThreshold=4
;      ---- frame cap, added with the 52% drop ----
;      t.MaxFPS=30 is what turns spare headroom into STABILITY instead of a wobble.
;      Uncapped, ~36 avg would swing 31-40 and the frame times would be uneven; a
;      30 cap holds a flat frame every time the GPU can make it, and the panel's
;      60 Hz virtual base makes 30 an exact half-refresh, so pacing is even too.
;      Verified registered in the shipping exe. This outranks the game's own
;      FrameRateLimit=0 (SetByGameSetting loses to SetBySystemSettingsIni).
;      MEASURED INERT 2026-08-11 - the claim above is wrong in practice. At
