r.PostProcessAAQuality=4
;      ---- GEN 5 TAA - OPTION, NOT APPLIED. Added 2026-09-11. Verified registered. ----
;      r.TemporalAA.Algorithm 1 selects the experimental Gen 5 TAA that shipped in 4.26/4.27 and
;      became TSR in UE5. It exists specifically to upsample better: sharper reconstruction and
;      less ghosting at exactly this kind of 60 % render scale, which is where every pixel on
;      screen comes from. NOT cheap - several compute passes at output resolution - and it is
;      the same class of compute-heavy screen-space pass that SSGI was when it crashed the game
;      in August. Test alone: if it crashes at start or in play, put it back to 0; if it runs,
;      read the counter and decide whether the sharper image is worth what it costs.
;      LIVE 2026-09-11 at his request. It matters MORE now than when it was written: with dynamic
;      resolution on, the render fraction floats, so a better reconstruction filter does not just
;      look sharper - it lets the same frame budget hold a higher fraction. Failure signature is
;      its own and nothing else here shares it: a crash at start or in play, or visible ghosting
;      and smearing behind moving mutants. Either one, set this back to 0.
;      *** REVERTED 2026-09-11, THE SAME DAY. It measured 25-27 -> 18-20 fps and the two cuts it
;      shipped with can only REMOVE work, so this is the cause with no ambiguity.
;      THE MECHANISM IS THE IMPORTANT PART, because it is a general rule now: Gen 5 TAA runs its
;      passes at OUTPUT resolution - 5120x1440 here - not at render resolution. So its cost does
;      NOT shrink when dynamic resolution lowers the render fraction. It is a fixed toll on every
;      frame that dynamic resolution cannot pay down, which is why the picture got slower AND
;      softer at once: the heuristic kept cutting render fraction and never recovered the time.
;      GENERAL RULE: with dynamic resolution on, any full-OUTPUT-resolution pass is far more
;      expensive than it looks, because it is the one cost the system cannot trade away.
;      Do not re-enable this while the output is 5120x1440.
;r.TemporalAA.Algorithm=0
; ---- image quality that survives upscaling (all near-free) ----
;      Sharpen 1.5 -> 1.0 on 2026-08-11. 1.5 was tuned when the game rendered at
;      native, where the sharpen pass had real pixels to work with. It now runs
;      AFTER TAAU has upscaled from 60%, so it sharpens interpolated pixels and
;      can ring - a pale outline on the dark side of high-contrast edges (pipe
;      against wall, HUD text against panel). Put it back to 1.5 if 1.0 reads
;      soft; ringing is the thing to watch for, not softness.
;      THIS IS THE ONLY r.Tonemapper.Sharpen LINE; UE4 silently takes the last
;      occurrence, so do not add a second one below.
;      MipMapLODBias is back to 0 from -0.5: a negative bias costs texture
;      bandwidth on unified memory and aliases badly once you are upscaling.
;      Anisotropy 16 -> 8: at 60% render scale the two are near-indistinguishable
;      and 8 spends less of the bandwidth the GPU is actually short of.
;      RE-APPLIED 1.0 -> 1.5, the only BEAUTY line kept through this restore, because it is free:
;      taps inside the tonemapper pass that already runs, no new pass and no new render target.
;      Watch for ringing (a pale outline on the dark side of a high-contrast edge), not softness.
;      *** 1.5 -> 1.1 ON 2026-09-11 - FREE, AND IT SHOULD LOOK BETTER, NOT WORSE. ***
;      1.5 was restored earlier today with an explicit condition: that the game was rendering at
;      native, because the sharpen runs AFTER the upscale. That condition no longer holds - the
;      render scale is 50 %, so this sharpens pixels TAAU interpolated, which is precisely the case
;      this file has warned about since August. The failure mode is ringing: a pale outline on the
;      dark side of a high-contrast edge, a pipe against a wall, HUD text against a panel.
;      Free either way - it is taps inside the tonemapper pass that already runs. 1.0 if any
;      outline is still visible, 1.3 if it now reads soft.
r.Tonemapper.Sharpen=1.1
r.Tonemapper.Quality=5
;      ---- AUTO HDR SUPPORT, added 2026-09-08. Engine HDR is dead on this GPU (see the HDR block
;      below), so Windows Auto HDR is the HDR path now: it inverse-tonemaps the game's finished
;      8-bit SDR frame. That changes what is worth tuning. Auto HDR AMPLIFIES whatever is in the
;      SDR image, so the thing to protect is the SDR image's smoothness in the DARK, because
;      this game is 90% dark corridors and stretching an 8-bit dark gradient is exactly where
;      banding appears.
;      GrainQuantization=1 is the dither UE4 applies before quantising the tonemapper output to
;      8 bits. It is the single most Auto-HDR-relevant line available and it is FREE - it is a
;      few instructions at the end of a pass that already runs. Pinned here rather than left to
;      the default so it cannot be lost. NOTE this is dither, NOT film grain; film grain is a
;      post-process volume property and is still unreachable from any ini.
r.Tonemapper.GrainQuantization=1
;      >>> MEASURED INERT 2026-09-07 - BOTH LINES BELOW. Do not tune them again. <<<
;      Scanning the 7.5 GB base pak found 2,951 individual texture assets carrying an explicit
;      Filter=TF_Nearest override (against 7 bilinear and 3 trilinear). The DeviceProfile
;      TextureLODGroups all say MinMagFilter=aniso, but a per-asset Filter BEATS the group, so
;      nearly every surface in the station is point-sampled no matter what these two say.
;      Point filtering is deliberate art direction by Nightdive, it is set per asset, and NO ini
;      can reach it - only a pak that replaces the texture .uasset headers (Nexus mod 21).
;      That mod was declined 2026-09-07: it is cooked against the June 2023 v1.0 assets and this
;      is the 2026-07-20 build, so its headers would be paired with base-pak .ubulk data.
;      Consequence: these lines are kept only so they are correct IF that mod ever goes in. If it
;      does, r.MaxAnisotropy becomes live for the first time and is then the line to drop to 4.
r.MipMapLODBias=0
;      8 -> 16 ON 2026-09-08, and for the FIRST TIME this line does something: the texture
;      filtering pak (NVEnableTextureFiltering) replaced the TF_Nearest overrides, so anisotropy
;      is finally live. Anisotropy is exactly the filtering that matters here - it sharpens
;      surfaces viewed at a grazing angle, which in a first-person game means FLOORS and long
;      corridors, i.e. most of what is on screen in Citadel. 8 -> 16 doubles the taps on those
;      surfaces only; head-on surfaces are unaffected.
;      Affordable because the batch that made this line live (filtering pak + motion blur + scene
;      fringe) measured at 23 fps, i.e. FREE - so there is texture-bandwidth headroom to spend.
;      This is the most on-taste line in the file: he wants realism, not pixelation.
;      Drop to 8 if frames fall, then 4. Do NOT drop the mod - the mod is the whole point.
r.MaxAnisotropy=16
;      FilterSize 1.0 -> 0.9 on 2026-09-07. This is the spatial kernel TAA uses when it
;      resolves its history, i.e. it sharpens the RECONSTRUCTION, where Tonemapper.Sharpen
;      above sharpens the already-upscaled result. Doing it here is strictly better: it is
;      the direct answer to the ringing warning on the Sharpen line, because real detail
;      recovered at resolve time needs less faking afterwards. Free - it changes sample
;      weights in a pass that already runs, no extra work. Verified registered in the exe.
;      If the image starts to shimmer or crawl on slow pans, go back to 1.0; that is the
;      failure mode, not softness. 0.8 is available if 0.9 still reads soft.
r.TemporalAAFilterSize=0.9

; ---- REAL HDR: the engine's own HDR output, not Windows Auto HDR ----
;      Added 2026-08-11. All five verified registered in the shipping exe.
;      OutputDevice 3 = ACES 1000-nit ST-2084/PQ, matching the G95NC's
;      DisplayHDR 1000 / calibrated ~1000-nit peak. ColorGamut 1 = DCI-P3.
;      UI.CompositeMode 1 keeps the HUD tone-mapped separately so it does not
;      blow out. UI.Level raises HUD brightness if it reads dim in HDR.
;      Requires: Windows HDR ON for the panel (Win+Alt+B) and Windows Auto HDR
;      OFF for this exe - two HDR passes stacked look wrong.
;      VERDICT 2026-08-11: engine HDR is OFF. The scene looked great, but the HUD
;      became unreadable and r.HDR.UI.Level=3.0 changed NOTHING - proof the HUD does
;      ENGINE HDR ON 2026-09-08 on request, overriding the 2026-08-11 verdict
;      above. The HUD blowout was expected to come back exactly as it did in August, and
;      it was wanted anyway. Everything below this point is unchanged from the August attempt.
;      MITIGATION TO TRY, since r.HDR.UI.Level is inert here: the in-game brightness/gamma
;      slider darkens the HUD along with the scene, because the HUD is composited into scene
;      colour BEFORE the tonemapper (through the game's own MAT_UI_ScreenEffects material,
;      which is why no r.HDR.UI.* cvar can reach it).
;      REQUIRED, and it is a manual step: Windows Auto HDR must be OFF for this exe or two
;      HDR passes stack. Settings > System > Display > Graphics > SystemReShock > Options.
;      TO REVERT: put these two lines back to 0. Nothing else needs touching.
;      >>> ATTEMPT 3, 8 September 2026: scRGB. THIS IS THE COMMUNITY RECIPE. <<<
;      Attempts 1 (11 August) and 2 (8 September, morning) both used OutputDevice=3, ACES
;      1000-nit ST-2084/PQ, and both washed the UI out - grey, unreadable inventory and menus.
;      The recipe people actually run for this game is OutputDevice=5 + ColorGamut=2, and the
;      difference is not brightness, it is ENCODING. Device 3 is PQ: the whole 0-1 range is
;      remapped onto an absolute-nit curve, so UI drawn at sRGB 0-1 lands wherever ACES puts it.
;      Device 5 is scRGB linear FP16: 0-1 stays SDR sRGB and only values ABOVE 1.0 become HDR
;      highlights - so a UI drawn at 0-1 keeps SDR paper white by construction. Same 1000-nit
;      target as device 3, so this is not a brightness gamble.
;      Gamut 2 = Rec.2020 (was 1 = DCI-P3), which is what the scRGB recipe pairs with.
;      NOTE the earlier warning in this file against devices 4 and 6 conflated encoding with the
;      nit target: 4 and 6 are the 2000-nit variants and are still not wanted, but 5 was never
;      the same thing as 4/6 and should have been tried first.
;      Known residual artefact from community reports even with this recipe: 2D inventory ICON
;      textures can still read washed out. If that happens, the ladder is:
;        1. r.HDR.UI.CompositeMode=0   (never tried; skips the separate UI luminance pass)
;        2. r.HDR.UI.Level 3.0 -> 1.0  (3.0 was measured inert with device 3, retest on 5)
;        3. give up on engine HDR and go back to Windows Auto HDR (set the two lines to 0)
;      No native HDR menu exists to fall back on: an exe string scan on 8 September 2026 found
;      only stock UE4 API names (HDRDisplayOutputNits, GetCurrentHDRDisplayNits) and no menu
;      text - the Display HDR option present in the 2021 demo was cut from the release build.
r.AllowHDR=0
r.HDR.EnableHDROutput=0
;      >>> DEVICE 5 -> 3 ON 2026-09-08. LAST CLEAN TEST OF ENGINE HDR. <<<
;      Device 5 (ACES 1000-nit scRGB) needs the swap chain to be DXGI_FORMAT_R16G16B16A16_FLOAT
;      in the scRGB colour space. Device 3 (ACES 1000-nit ST-2084/PQ) needs R10G10B10A2 in the
;      PQ colour space instead. If the Adreno driver under Prism will not create one of those
;      two, UE4 falls back to an SDR swap chain but the TONEMAPPER keeps encoding for whatever
;      OutputDevice says - and HDR pixels in an SDR surface is exactly the grey picture. So the
;      swap-chain FORMAT is the variable worth changing, and 10-bit PQ is far more widely
;      supported than FP16 scRGB on mobile-derived GPUs.
;      Neither device has ever had a CLEAN test: every device-3 attempt ran with
;      bUseHDRDisplayOutput already reset to False by a previous exit, and the one device-5
;      success was the single launch right after that flag was first written.
;      Gamut back to 1 (DCI-P3), which is what pairs with PQ on the G95NC.
;      IF THIS IS ALSO GREY: engine HDR is out on this hardware. Set AllowHDR=0,
;      EnableHDROutput=0 AND OutputDevice=0 - all three, or the grey stays - and use Special K.
r.HDR.Display.OutputDevice=0
r.HDR.Display.ColorGamut=0
r.HDR.UI.CompositeMode=1
;      UI.Level 3.0 -> 1.0 (ENGINE DEFAULT) on 8 September 2026, after he reported HDR working
;      on device 5 with the inventory icons washed out. This cvar is a LUMINANCE MULTIPLIER for
;      UI elements composited into the HDR framebuffer. It was set to 3.0 in August to brighten
;      a HUD that read dim under device 3, and it was measured inert there - but under scRGB it
;      is not inert and it is actively wrong: on device 5 the value 1.0 already IS SDR paper
;      white, so a 3x multiplier drives UI whites to roughly 300 nits. Washed out is exactly
;      what that looks like. 1.0 puts the UI back at paper white.
;      ONE CHANGE AT A TIME: if the icons are still washed out after this, the next single step
;      is r.HDR.UI.CompositeMode=0. Do not change both at once - CompositeMode 0 removes the
;      luminance-preserving blend for the WHOLE UI and can regress text that currently reads fine.
;      If neither helps, it is not fixable from an ini: the inventory icons in this game are very
;      likely scene captures of the item meshes rather than flat 2D art, and a capture render
;      target does not go through the UI composite path at all.
;      REVERTED TO 3.0 the same day at his call: he preferred how it looked at 3.0 and accepts
;      the washed-out inventory icons. So the multiplier is NOT the icon cause after all, or he
;      simply likes the brighter HUD - either way 3.0 is the setting he wants. Do not "fix" this
;      again. If the icons are ever revisited, r.HDR.UI.CompositeMode=0 is the untried step.
r.HDR.UI.Level=3.0

; ---- RAM: texture streaming ----
;      PoolSize 6144 -> 3072 and FullyLoadUsedTextures dropped. On this SoC the
;      GPU shares LPDDR5X bandwidth with the CPU, so forcing full mips resident
;      for every used texture spends the one resource the GPU is starved of.
;      LimitPoolSizeToVRAM=0 still lets the pool draw from system RAM.
;      Raised 3072 -> 4096 on 2026-08-11: pop-in is an immersion cost and pool size
;      4096 -> 6144 on 2026-08-12 by choice, with RAM measured free and the
;      GPU pegged at 100%. NOTE the older warning in this file said never to go back to
;      6144 because that value was in the 6-7 fps config - but the line that actually
;      cost those frames was r.Streaming.FullyLoadUsedTextures=1, which forced full mips
;      resident for every used texture and is still gone. Pool size alone only sets a
;      budget; it does not force anything resident. Worst case on this unified-memory
;      SoC is more LPDDR5X bandwidth contention, so if the counter drops, this reverts.
;      6144 -> 8192 on 2026-08-27 (RAM-for-disk trade, 31.6 GB fitted): a bigger budget means
;      far textures stay resident instead of being dropped and re-streamed. Budget only.
r.Streaming.PoolSize=8192
; is paid in RAM (31 GB here), not in frames. Do NOT go back to 6144 - that was
;      part of the config that ran at 6-7 fps.
r.Streaming.LimitPoolSizeToVRAM=0
r.Streaming.MaxTempMemoryAllowed=4096
r.Streaming.HiddenPrimitiveScale=1
;      CPU-for-pop-in, 2026-08-27: streaming metrics refreshed every 3 frames instead of 5,
;      and 200 static components (not 50) re-evaluated per frame. Game-thread cost only; the
;      CPU has the headroom. Textures reach full mips sooner after turning a corner.
;      BOTH BACK TO ENGINE DEFAULTS (5 and 50) ON 2026-09-11. They were raised on 2026-08-27 as a
;      "CPU for pop-in" trade, on the reading that the CPU had headroom - true of the CPU as a
;      whole, but this is per-frame work on a thread that today's measurements identify as the
;      actual bottleneck. Reverting costs only slightly slower texture mip updates after turning a
;      corner; it costs no image quality at all.
;      Smaller effect than the prepass above, and a different thread, so it will not confuse the
;      attribution of that change.
;      ALSO NO CHANGE, so the 2026-08-27 values go back on 2026-09-11. They were bought to reduce
;      texture pop-in after turning a corner, the measurement says they cost nothing, so there is
;      no reason to keep the engine defaults and lose that.
;      *** BOTH BACK TO ENGINE DEFAULTS (5 and 50) ON 2026-09-11 - also aimed at the movement dip.
;      These were raised on 2026-08-27 to cut texture pop-in, and they are GAME-THREAD work whose
;      cost scales with how many components change relevance per frame - which is near zero standing
;      still and at its maximum when sprinting through the station. Re-evaluating 200 static
;      components every 3 frames instead of 50 every 5 is roughly 6x the churn precisely when the
;      frame is already tight.
;      An earlier test today showed no change in AVERAGE fps from this, which was the wrong metric:
;      it was never an average cost, it is a movement cost - the exact thing being fixed here.
;      Cost of reverting: textures reach full sharpness slightly later after turning a corner.
;      BOTH BACK TO 3 / 200 ON 2026-09-11, same reason: no measured effect on the dips, and the
;      engine defaults only cost later texture sharpening after a corner. Twice now this pair has
;      measured as nothing - treat it as settled and do not cut it again.
r.Streaming.FramesForFullUpdate=3
r.Streaming.NumStaticComponentsProcessedPerFrame=200

; ---- DISK: PSO + shader caches. Free, and they are why revisiting an area
;      never re-stutters. Untouched by the perf reset.
r.ShaderPipelineCache.Enabled=1
r.ShaderPipelineCache.StartupMode=1
r.ShaderPipelineCache.SaveAfterPSOsLogged=25
D3D12.PSO.DiskCache=1
D3D12.PSO.DriverOptimizedDiskCache=1
;      2026-08-27: precompile the recorded PSOs in bigger batches with more time per frame
;      (defaults 50 / 16 ms). Longer loading screens, fewer first-encounter stutters in play.
;      *** RE-APPLIED 2026-09-11 after the restore: the ONE find of the day that should ADD frames.
;      Precompile* runs during the LOADING SCREEN (free) and stays at 200 / 50 ms. The plain pair
;      governs the background batch DURING PLAY, and 50 ms of compile budget on a machine whose
;      whole frame is ~40 ms could halve the frame rate wherever PSO work is pending. 16 ms is the
;      engine default. This has never been measured alone - it is the best hope in this file.
r.ShaderPipelineCache.BatchSize=50
r.ShaderPipelineCache.PrecompileBatchSize=200
r.ShaderPipelineCache.BatchTime=16
r.ShaderPipelineCache.PrecompileBatchTime=50
;      Create shader objects when a material loads, not on first draw. Load-time CPU + RAM
;      for no shader-creation hitches mid-fight.
r.CreateShadersOnLoad=1

; ---- DISK: dedicated async asset-loading thread ----
s.AsyncLoadingThreadEnabled=1
s.AsyncLoadingUseFullTimeLimit=0

; ================================================================
;  GAME THREAD - the last untested subsystem, 2026-09-11.
;
;  WHY WE ARE HERE. He removed -norhithread and the frame rate did not change, which kills the
;  render-thread theory this file spent the day building. Put together with everything else
;  measured today - render fraction 40 vs 100 barely matters, draw calls measured free four times,
;  removing the whole depth prepass did nothing, shadow maps and AA are worth ~1 fps - almost
;  nothing on the GPU or the render thread moves this frame.
;  That leaves the GAME THREAD: UE4 gameplay work, animation, Blueprint VM, actor ticking, all of
;  it x64 code running under Prism emulation. Nothing in this file has ever touched it.
;  If these four lines do not move the number either, the limit is emulation overhead itself and
;  30 fps is not reachable from any config - that is a real answer, not a failure.
; ================================================================
;      ---- FREE: no visual change from either of these two. ----
;      ParallelAnimUpdate defaults to 0, meaning ALL animation update work - anim Blueprint logic
;      for every mutant, cyborg, corpse and the Hacker's own arms - runs on the game thread. 1 pushes
;      it across the task graph onto the 11 cores sitting idle. This is the single best "move work to
;      cheap resources" line left, and it is exactly the trade this machine wants.
;      Failure mode is animation misbehaving (pops, T-poses) if an anim Blueprint is not thread-safe.
a.ParallelAnimUpdate=1
a.ParallelAnimEvaluation=1
;      Async loading is allowed 5 ms of GAME-THREAD time per frame by default. On a ~43 ms frame that
;      is over 10 % handed to streaming whenever anything is loading. 2 ms gives most of it back; the
;      cost is textures and assets arriving a little later after a door opens.
;      BACK TO THE ENGINE DEFAULTS ON 2026-09-11: no measured gain, and 2 ms only made assets arrive
;      later after a door opens. .ParallelAnimUpdate/.ParallelAnimEvaluation above are KEPT -
;      unlike these, they cost nothing at all, and moving animation onto idle cores can only help in
;      a busy fight even if a quiet corridor cannot show it.
s.AsyncLoadingTimeLimit=5

; ---- CPU: parallel render-command generation. This is the half of the machine
;      with headroom (223 vs 150), so it stays on - except the thread lag below.
;      OneFrameThreadLag stays at 1. It was flipped to 0 on 2026-08-11 to trade
;      throughput for input latency, and reverted the same day by choice:
;      losing fps is not acceptable here. With it at 1 the game thread runs a frame
;      ahead of the render thread, which costs ~37 ms of input delay at 27 fps but
;      lets the two threads overlap. DO NOT set this to 0 again -
;      it reliably costs frames on this machine, which is the wrong side of the
;      trade here.

;      RHI THREAD OFF 2026-08-27. Four identical crashes on 2026-08-27 (00:31, 00:32, 01:08,
;      01:16), reproducible by approaching a locked door; mods ruled out (same crash with
;      InstantCardSwipes deleted) and the parallel cvars below ruled out (same crash with
;      all of them at 0). Every minidump shows the same stack on the dedicated RHI thread
;      (FRHIThread::Run -> ProcessThreadUntilRequestReturn(RHIThread) -> FExecuteRHIThreadTask
;      -> FRHICommandListExecutor::ExecuteInner -> FD3D12CommandContext resource transitions
;      -> virtual call on an already-freed D3D12 resource, DEP execute fault at a heap
;      address). With the RHI thread disabled the command lists execute on the render thread
;      before the resource can be released. Costs fps. The parallel cvars are back at their
;      original values because they were not involved.
;      If the door still crashes with this at 0, nothing else inside the D3D12 path is left
;      to switch off from the ini.
;      2026-08-27 01:18: r.RHIThread.Enable=0 in this section made the game crash at start:
;      "Console object named r.RHIThread.Enable can't be replaced with the new one of
;      different type" - in this build it is a console COMMAND, so it cannot be set from an
;      ini. The RHI thread is disabled with the Steam launch option -norhithread instead.
;      (Do not put r.RHIThread.Enable in this file.)
r.RHICmdUseParallelAlgorithms=1
r.RHICmdBypass=0
r.ParallelPrePass=1
r.ParallelBasePass=1
r.ParallelShadows=1
r.ParallelTranslucency=1
r.MeshDrawCommands.DynamicInstancing=1
;      RE-APPLIED pins. Both are already the engine default, so no change is expected - they are the
;      two mechanisms that keep draw-call work OFF the render thread (cached commands reuse earlier
;      frames' work; parallel setup builds them on the idle task threads), and a scalability change
;      silently dropping either would be the worst possible regression on this machine.
r.MeshDrawCommands.UseCachedCommands=1
r.MeshDrawCommands.ParallelPassSetup=1
r.OneFrameThreadLag=1
r.GTSyncType=1
;      2026-08-27: split parallel command lists every 32 draws instead of 64, so more of the
;      12 cores translate RHI commands at once and the render thread (which now also runs
;      the RHI work) has less to do itself.
;      RE-APPLIED: 32 -> 64 (engine default). Smaller batches mean MORE command lists, and each one
;      costs begin/end and state-restore work at EXECUTION - which since -norhithread is serial on
;      the render thread, the measured bottleneck. Zero visual change.
r.RHICmdMinDrawsPerParallelCmdList=64

; ---- culling: hierarchical Z instead of hardware occlusion queries ----
;      Added in the perf reset. Occlusion queries make the CPU wait on GPU
;      results, and that round-trip is exactly what emulation punishes.
r.HZBOcclusion=1

; ---- DEPTH PREPASS: the last big DRAW-CALL saving, and it costs NOTHING to look at. ----
;      Added 2026-09-11. Verified registered. The cooked project setting is r.EarlyZPass=2, i.e.
;      every opaque AND masked mesh is drawn TWICE per frame: once depth-only to prime the depth
;      buffer, then again for real. The prepass exists to stop the GPU shading pixels that later
;      get covered up.
;      WHY IT IS THE RIGHT THING TO CUT HERE, and this is the whole lesson of today applied:
;        1. It is pure DRAW-CALL cost, which is the class this machine is actually limited by -
;           the render thread translates every one of those calls through Prism emulation.
;        2. What it BUYS is saved GPU shading, and this machine has GPU to spare: cutting 36 % of
;           the pixels earlier bought exactly one frame.
;        3. The Adreno X1-85 is a tile-based GPU with hardware low-resolution-Z occlusion, so it
;           already does in hardware most of what the prepass does in software. On this GPU
;           specifically the pass is closer to redundant than it would be on a desktop part.
;      So it trades work the machine cannot afford for work it has spare. ZERO visual difference -
;      the final image is identical, only the route to it changes.
;      IF THIS BACKFIRES the symptom is the opposite of a draw-call win: the frame rate falls
;      instead of rising, because overdraw now costs more than the calls saved. Middle step is
;      r.EarlyZPass=1 (opaque only, skipping the expensive masked meshes); 2 restores the cooked
;      behaviour. The DBuffer feature that would REQUIRE a prepass is off in the cooked config
;      (r.DBuffer=False), so nothing depends on this.
;      MEASURED: NO CHANGE (24-26 fps before and after), so BACK TO THE COOKED VALUE 2 on
;      2026-09-11. The reasoning for cutting it was sound and the measurement simply says draw
;      calls are not the limit either. Restoring it is the safe default: the shipped path is the
;      tested one, and without a prepass a heavy-overdraw room could cost frames later even though
;      the test scene did not. Keeping a change that buys nothing is how a config rots.
r.EarlyZPass=2

; ---- STABILITY: free, added 2026-08-27 alongside -norhithread ----
;      ResizeMethod 2 = render targets only ever GROW. Default 0 reallocates them on every
;      resolution change (menu <-> game, cyberspace), which is exactly the create/destroy
;      churn behind the door crash. Costs a little RAM once, never frames.
r.SceneRenderTargetResizeMethod=2
;      RE-APPLIED: the cooked tier-3 render-target pool floor. Keeps intermediate buffers allocated
;      instead of freed and recreated as the pass mix changes; allocation is render-thread work,
;      holding memory is not. RAM for frames, same trade as the shadow cache above.
r.RenderTargetPoolMin=400
;      Cap cached point/spot shadow-map refreshes at 2 per frame (default -1 = unlimited).
;      Spreads the cost of many lights changing at once over a few frames instead of one
;      spike; average fps unchanged, frame pacing smoother in busy rooms.
;      *** 2 -> 1 ON 2026-09-11, FOR THE FLOOR, NOT THE AVERAGE. ***
;      These cap how many cached point/spot shadow maps may be re-rendered in a single frame. They
;      cost nothing on average - the same maps are refreshed either way - they just refuse to do
;      several in one frame. A point light's shadow is a SIX-FACE cubemap, so two of them landing on
;      the same frame is a large, sudden spike, and this station is full of flickering lights.
;      This is the purest stability line available: it converts a dip into a one-frame-late shadow.
r.Shadow.MaxNumPointShadowCacheUpdatesPerFrame=1
r.Shadow.MaxNumSpotShadowCacheUpdatesPerFrame=1

; ---- CPU/RAM: fewer GC hitches + cached static shadows ----
gc.TimeBetweenPurgingPendingKillObjects=120
r.Shadow.CacheWholeSceneShadows=1

; ---- SHADOWS: where most of the 6-7 fps went ----
;      RadiusThreshold 0.0 was the worst line in the old file. It removed the
;      small-caster cull, so every piece of clutter was re-rendered once per
;      shadow-casting light in range - and LightMaxDrawDistanceScale=4 had
;      widened "in range" fourfold at the same time. Back to the 0.03 default.
;      4096 maps at DistanceScale 2.0 also overflowed the whole-scene shadow
;      cache every frame, quietly turning the cache above into a per-frame
;      re-render. 2048 at scale 1.0 fits in 256 MB and actually caches.
;      *** 3 -> 5 ON 2026-09-11. NOT a beauty add - a CORRECTION. ***
