;      kernel per shadowed pixel (blocker search, then the variable-width filter) instead of one.
;      TUNE BEFORE REVERTING: r.Shadow.MaxSoftKernelSize (default 40) only becomes live with PCSS
;      and it CAPS the widest filter, so dropping it to 24 or 16 keeps contact-hardening while
;      cutting the worst-case cost. Try that before going back to FilterMethod 0.
;      Verified registered in the shipping exe 2026-09-08. Expect this to cost real frames -
;      it is the one line here that has never had a plausible free-lunch argument.
;      *** PCSS OFF 2026-09-11 to hit a stable 30. This file measured it at 4 fps (27 -> 23) and
;      called it the most expensive line after ScreenPercentage - two passes over the kernel per
;      shadowed pixel instead of one. It is the single biggest saving available that is not
;      resolution, which is exactly what a 30 fps target needs.
;      THIS IS A REAL LOSS AND IT IS THE ONE TO BUY BACK FIRST: shadow edges go back to a uniform
;      softness, so a crate no longer has a hard edge where it meets the floor and a soft one
;      where its shadow is thrown across the corridor. Set it to 1 again the moment there is
;      headroom - it is the best-looking line in the file.
;      r.Shadow.MaxSoftKernelSize below is PCSS-only and is now inert; it is left so that setting
;      FilterMethod back to 1 restores the tuned version rather than stock.
;      PCSS RESTORED 2026-09-11 after the pinned-40 % test. It was cut this morning for 4 fps that
