;      2048 -> 4096 on 2026-08-12, add-back queue item 5. This is the RAM-for-GPU trade
;      asked for when the profile came back GPU 100% / CPU + RAM idle: the whole-scene
;      shadow cache holds rendered shadow maps in RAM so static casters are NOT
;      re-rendered every frame. Bigger maps need a bigger cache or the cache silently
;      stops caching and every frame re-renders - which is exactly what made the old
;      6-7 fps config so slow. 4096 maps are 4x the texels of 2048, so the cache goes
;      256 -> 512 MB WITH them. These three lines move together, never separately.
;      Nothing else here can shift load off the GPU: rasterisation cannot run on the CPU,
;      so caching is the only genuine GPU->RAM trade available.
;      FIRST THING TO REVERT if the counter drops - 4x shadow texels is the single most
;      expensive line in this file after ScreenPercentage.
;      4096 -> 2048 ON 2026-09-08, AND THE CACHE WITH IT (1024 -> 512 MB). This reverses the
;      2026-08-12 add-back, and the argument for reversing it is a resolution argument, not a
;      taste one: the scene renders at 3072x864 (ScreenPercentage 60 of 5120x1440), so a 4096
;      shadow map carries roughly 4 shadow texels per rendered screen pixel. Every one of those
;      extra texels is rasterised, filtered and cached, and TAAU then throws the detail away in
;      the upscale. 2048 is still ~1 texel per screen pixel here, which is the point where more
;      resolution stops being visible. This was the file's own second-most-expensive line.
;      Put all three back to 4096/4096/1024 together if the shadows ever read blocky.
;      *** 2048 -> 1024 ON 2026-09-11, AND THE CACHE WITH THEM (512 -> 256 MB). These three always
;      move together - a cache sized for bigger maps than it holds silently stops caching.
;      WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT CUT NOW, and it is a NEW argument that did not exist before dynamic
;      resolution: shadow maps are rendered at a FIXED size. They do not shrink when the heuristic
;      lowers the render fraction. So while every pixel cost is being traded away automatically,
;      shadow rasterisation keeps charging the same amount every frame - which is exactly why the
;      frame rate stalls at 26-28 instead of reaching the 30 the budget asks for.
;      WHY IT IS CHEAP TO LOSE: the scene now renders between 40 % and 70 % of 5120x1440, so 2048
;      maps were carrying well over one shadow texel per screen pixel at the top of that range and
;      several at the bottom - detail the upscale discards. And with PCSS off, every shadow edge is
;      uniformly soft anyway, which is the best possible cover for a lower-resolution map.
;      Put all three back to 2048/2048/512 together if shadows read blocky or stair-stepped.
;      BACK TO 2048 ON 2026-09-11 with the cache, for the same reason as PCSS above: shadow-map
;      rasterisation is GPU work, and the GPU is not the limit. PCSS also needs the texels - the
;      blocker search reads the map, so a 1024 map makes contact hardening mushy.
;      *** 2048 -> 1024 AGAIN ON 2026-09-11, and this time with the RIGHT reason. ***
;      A shadow map is rendered at a FIXED size. It does not shrink when the render fraction drops,
;      so it is charged in full every frame no matter what the resolution ceiling says - and 2048
;      is FOUR TIMES the texels of 1024 to rasterise, filter and cache.
;      That is why the ceiling barely mattered: 100 % -> 22-23 fps and 80 % -> 23-24 fps, so
;      cutting 36 % of the PIXELS bought one frame. The cost was never in the pixels.
;      PCSS STAYS ON. It is per-shadowed-pixel work, which scales with the render fraction and is
;      therefore in the cheap class; it just does its blocker search against a smaller map, so
;      contact edges are slightly softer. That is a far better trade than losing PCSS entirely.
r.Shadow.MaxResolution=1024
